{
  "dom_id": "step-q77826",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14193272/assets/70e89048ce23f129f9b79da6aa836ce6dd4cdb4658a2b31c964678086b5b9649.png)\n\nWhat is the global minimum of the function shown above?",
  "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value that the function attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point of a function is a point $(x,y)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x)=-7.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-7.$ The function reaches this value at $x=-12.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14193272/assets/a3c4dc5c83239ec0ea86a1585f6d05307d1b13d12d9170e1b2bd39e5bd5283a1.png)",
